data.content:
**Yes, C# fully supports `stackalloc` into a `Span` of native-sized integers.** Both `Span<nint>` and `Span<nuint>` work:

```csharp
Span<nint>  signed   = stackalloc nint[4];
Span<nuint> unsigned = stackalloc nuint[4];
```

On this 64-bit machine, each element is 8 bytes (they'd be 4 bytes on a 32-bit runtime). The only gotcha encountered was that `sizeof(nint)` requires an `unsafe` context since the size isn't fixed at compile time — but `stackalloc` itself works without `unsafe` when assigned directly to a `Span`.
